{
"Good A": "Person 1",
"Good B": "Person 3",
"Good C": "Person 2",
"Good D": "None"}
}

The allocation that I consider to be the fairest is the one that gives each person the good that they value the most. In this case, Person 1 values Good A the most, Person 3 values Good B the most, Person 2 values Good C the most, and all three people value Good D the least. Therefore, the fairest allocation is to give Good A to Person 1, Good B to Person 3, Good C to Person 2, and discard Good D.

{
"Good A": "Person 2", "Good B": "Person 3", "Good C": "Person 1", "Good D": "None"
}
In this allocation, Person 1 gets Good C which they value the highest and Good A which they value as the second highest. Person 2 gets Good A which they value the highest. Person 3 gets Good B which they value the highest. Good D is not allocated to any person since none of them value it more than 7. This allocation is fair because it gives each person the good they value the most and gives the second most valued good to Person 1.
